"I fixed all six findings in both plans. Two fixes take a different form from the finding's wording, and the reasons are given below. Both plans have dated §8 entries. The slice-2 plan was changed only with small exact-string edits, and no line written by a builder was touched. The file went from 3151 to 3178 lines, which is exactly the 27 lines I added, so nothing written by another agent was lost.\n\nFiles:\n- /home/zhengyew/telelean/docs/slice2-plan.md\n- /tmp/claude-1000/-home-zhengyew-telelean/20626c17-0334-4498-ad86-9dc24b7010ed/scratchpad/plans/slice3-plan.md\n\nDispositions:\n\n**AP30-1 (major): fixed in both plans.** I checked the claim against the code:\n- G0's word list (`packages/core/src/gate/g0.ts`) refuses only `initialize` and `builtin_initialize`, not the `init` attribute.\n- In the 4.35 toolchain sources, `processHeaderCore` imports with `loadExts := true`, and `finalizePersistentExtensions` then calls `runInitAttrs`. For a non-module importer the IR phases are `.all`, so no `[init]` is skipped.\n- `importModules` defaults to `loadExts := false` and `level := .private`.\n- In the runner, `parseLeanJsonMessages` accepts any JSON line, and `parseCheckOutput` takes the first information message on a probe's line.\n- The sandbox leaves `<shadow>/.lake` writable.\n- G0 runs before any build (`runCheck`).\n\nSlice 2:\n- §1.1 2b2 gains a bullet: G0 refuses the `init` attribute.\n- §2.1 replaces the sentence the finding names. The probe-file outline now calls `importModules mods {} (loadExts := false) (level := .private)`.\n- A new §2.1 sub-bullet, \"No Claude code runs at import\", states the rule: `init` or `builtin_init`, matched by canonical name, wherever an attribute list names it. The plain identifier `init` stays allowed.\n- §6.1 2b2 gets the refused and allowed forms as tests.\n- §6.1 2b3a gets a new real-Lean overlay, `probe-init`: its probe job stays `ok` with the true constants. The control test shows the check job does print the injected line.\n- D2-75 and the §7.4 AP-entry text are updated.\n\nSlice 3:\n- §2.1 and D3-52 cite the G0 rule and `loadExts := false`.\n- Cheat 24 gains an `@[init]` variant that G0 refuses before any Lean job runs (built by hand in 3a).\n\n**AP30-2 (minor): fixed, adapted.**\n- (i) When B's probe failed, `checkBase` still writes base-facts.json and answers an `environment` outcome with `probeFailure`'s sentence plus \"Claude's work on this model cannot be checked.\". The start then refuses before anything is sent to Claude.\n  - I used `environment` instead of the finding's \"problem\". The start's text for problems says \"your files have Lean errors … fix or save them\", which is wrong for a probe failure. The environment text says \"nothing was sent to Claude\".\n- The size limit is logged, not removed. 2b3a reports the model size at which the probe's message reaches 32 MB. D2-75 states the limit and how to lift it.\n- Slice-2 sections touched: §2.1 (a new start sub-bullet, \"Compared approximately\", Cost), §1.1 2b3a, §6.1 2b3a tests, D2-75.\n- (ii) For slice 3 I took the finding's \"log it\" option. D3-52 now says that a meaning-walk failure or an oversized output fails the whole probe, slice 2's checks with it, and blocks the start. Keeping slice 2's constants when only the meaning part fails is logged as the alternative.\n  - 3p reports the model size at which the output would reach the cap. The person's `mental_model/` is the size of the Spyro fixture (89 lines against 88), so the Spyro measurement stands for it.\n  - §0's list now says 3p reads the probe's message line by line, constants line first, so 2b3a's parsing tests change.\n  - §2.1 \"Base facts\": the re-check replaces the file whatever `checkBase` answers, and a session whose B fails this way cannot go on; Exit ends it. §1.1 3p, §6.1 3p and §7.3 are updated to match.\n\n**AP30-3 (minor): fixed in slice 2.** I confirmed in `judge.ts` that `ok` includes \"no `facts` problem\" while `stepFindings` does not.\n- §1.1 2b3b: a session step's check, freeze and gate apply the failed-probe rule to `stepFindings` and never decide on `ok` alone.\n- The §2.9 SessionCheckContext comment and the step-purpose sub-bullet in §2.1 say the same.\n- 2b3b tests on a failed probe: session check, freeze_statements, proved finish and not-proved finish.\n- 2b3a's gate test adds gate-not-proved.\n\n**AP30-4 (minor): fixed in both plans.**\n- Slice 2 (§3.8, §2.9 `writePremise`, §1.1 2b3b, tests in 2b3b and 2f): a premise write whose judgement's probe failed is taken back out. The item stays accepted and its write stays due. The stage problem's error is `probeFailure`'s sentence. So step 4 never records a key from a judgement missing its constants.\n- Slice 3:\n  - §5.2 has a new bullet, \"Only from a judgement with every entry\": the upgrade sets `frozen` and removes `upgrade` only when the judgement has a `freezeAtAccept` entry for every accepted record. Otherwise the upgrade stays pending. The §2.2 `upgrade` doc says the same.\n  - §3.7: a premise write's step 4 always has its entry, because the slice-2 write fails on a failed probe.\n  - §2.3: the claim that no map is ever taken from facts whose probe failed is corrected. An unwritten premise's map is read from such facts, and its write's step 4 replaces it.\n  - New 3d test: an upgrade judge on a failed probe sets no `frozen` and keeps `upgrade`; the next working judge completes it.\n\n**AP30-5 (nit): fixed in both plans.**\n- (a) Slice 2: 2b2 adds `ItemClass.probeFailure`, set by classify. When the probe failed, the reducer's Accept refusal reads that sentence plus \"Decline what caused it, or Exit.\" (`REVIEW_TEXTS.acceptProbeFailed`), not ADV-3's \"its file did not build. Wait…\". Sections: §1.1, §2.1 stage sub-bullet, §2.2, §6.1 test.\n- (b) The `probeFailure` contract now says it returns undefined for `ok`, `oom`, `timeout`, `cancelled` and `could-not-start`.\n- (c) Slice 3: D3-51 and §3.5 state the exception for a partial stage judgement. §2.3 says slice 2's refusal comes first. The base ‹why› now reads \"the meaning check failed on your files: ‹cause›\", without \"reload\". The 3d test is updated.\n\n**AP30-6 (nit): fixed in both plans.**\n- (a) The reverses of D2-75 and D3-52 now both say that reversing the isolation exposes the meaning walk again.\n- (b) The `probe-forge` test in §6.1 3p is reworded as the finding proposes.\n- (c) Slice 2's ghost-check residual is stated as D2-75's own; the circular pointer to D3-52 is gone.\n\nNo finding was rejected. No code, git state, `dist` or `out` was touched, and no build, test or Lean job was run."